                <pb facs="0253" n="143" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg143"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/2010: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-08T20:00:00+08:00">October 8, 1931—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 8—6:45 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>729. Following information just received from [apparent omission] of Young
                    Marshal’s office: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Between 1 and 2:15 this p.m., 12 Japanese airplanes flew over Chinchow,
                            new seat of the Chinese Provincial Government in Manchuria and dropped
                            36 bombs, chiefly directly [<hi rendition="#italic">directed?</hi>] at
                            the University buildings and house [of the?] Provincial Government.
                            Buildings were damaged but casualties so far unknown. A bomb was dropped
                            on service car number 141, killing two Chinese cleaners, and <pb
                                facs="0254" n="144" xml:id="pg144"/> one was dropped on the
                            locomotive sheds. Telegraph lines between Chinchow and Shanhaikwan
                            interrupted.”</p></quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
